"[2511.09478] AdaCuRL: Adaptive Curriculum Reinforcement Learning with Invalid Sample Mitigation and Historical Revisiting (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09478)\nciteturn5974view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09478\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 165\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:AdaCuRL: Adaptive Curriculum Reinforcement Learning with Invalid Sample Mitigation and Historical Revisiting\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Renda Li , cite6†Hailang Huang , cite7†Fei Wei , cite8†Feng Xiong , cite9†Yong Wang , cite10†Xiangxiang Chu L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled AdaCuRL: Adaptive Curriculum Reinforcement Learning with Invalid Sample Mitigation and Historical Revisiting, by Renda Li and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Reinforcement learning (RL) has demonstrated considerable potential for enhancing reasoning in large language models (LLMs). However, existing methods suffer from Gradient Starvation and Policy Degradation when training directly on samples with mixed difficulty. To mitigate this, prior approaches leverage Chain-of-Thought (CoT) data, but the construction of high-quality CoT annotations remains labor-intensive.\nL17: Alternatively, curriculum learning strategies have been explored but frequently encounter challenges, such as difficulty mismatch, reliance on manual curriculum design, and catastrophic forgetting. To address these issues, we propose AdaCuRL, a Adaptive Curriculum Reinforcement Learning framework that integrates coarse-to-fine difficulty estimation with adaptive curriculum scheduling.\nL18: This approach dynamically aligns data difficulty with model capability and incorporates a data revisitation mechanism to mitigate catastrophic forgetting. Furthermore, AdaCuRL employs adaptive reference and sparse KL strategies to prevent Policy Degradation. Extensive experiments across diverse reasoning benchmarks demonstrate that AdaCuRL consistently achieves significant performance improvements on both LLMs and MLLMs.\nL19: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2511.09478 [cs.LG]\nL21:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2511.09478v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL22:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09478†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Renda Li [cite16†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 16:50:16 UTC (886 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled AdaCuRL: Adaptive Curriculum Reinforcement Learning with Invalid Sample Mitigation and Historical Revisiting, by Renda Li and 5 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite11†View PDF L35:   * cite12†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite17†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite18†view license L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.LG\nL42: \nL43: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L44: \nL45: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2025-11 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: cite24†cs L50: cite25†cs.AI L51: cite26†cs.CL L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite27†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite28†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite29†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite30†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Connected Papers (cite31†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL83: \nL84: Litmaps (cite32†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL85: \nL86: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL87: \nL88: scite Smart Citations (cite33†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai )\nL89: \nL90: [Input] Code, Data, Media\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.08832] TIGER-MARL: Enhancing Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Temporal Information through Graph-based Embeddings and Representations (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08832)\nciteturn5974view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08832\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 11 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:TIGER-MARL: Enhancing Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Temporal Information through Graph-based Embeddings and Representations\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Nikunj Gupta , cite6†Ludwika Twardecka , cite7†James Zachary Hare , cite8†Jesse Milzman , cite9†Rajgopal Kannan , cite10†Viktor Prasanna L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled TIGER-MARL: Enhancing Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Temporal Information through Graph-based Embeddings and Representations, by Nikunj Gupta and 5 other authors\nL14: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L15: > Abstract:In this paper, we propose capturing and utilizing \\textit{Temporal Information through Graph-based Embeddings and Representations} or \\textbf{TIGER} to enhance multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL). We explicitly model how inter-agent coordination structures evolve over time. While most MARL approaches rely on static or per-step relational graphs, they overlook the temporal evolution of interactions that naturally arise as agents adapt, move, or reorganize cooperation strategies.\nL16: Capturing such evolving dependencies is key to achieving robust and adaptive coordination. To this end, TIGER constructs dynamic temporal graphs of MARL agents, connecting their current and historical interactions. It then employs a temporal attention-based encoder to aggregate information across these structural and temporal neighborhoods, yielding time-aware agent embeddings that guide cooperative policy learning.\nL17: Through extensive experiments on two coordination-intensive benchmarks, we show that TIGER consistently outperforms diverse value-decomposition and graph-based MARL baselines in task performance and sample efficiency. Furthermore, we conduct comprehensive ablation studies to isolate the impact of key design parameters in TIGER, revealing how structural and temporal factors can jointly shape effective policy learning in MARL. All codes can be found here: cite13†this https URL†github.com .\nL18: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL19: Cite as:  | cite14†arXiv:2511.08832 [cs.LG]\nL20:    | (or cite15†arXiv:2511.08832v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL21:    | cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.08832†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL22: ## Submission history\nL23: \nL24: From: Nikunj Gupta [cite17†view email ]\nL25: [v1] Tue, 11 Nov 2025 23:00:23 UTC (5,396 KB)\nL26: \nL27: Full-text links:\nL28: \nL29: ## Access Paper:\nL30: \nL31: View a PDF of the paper titled TIGER-MARL: Enhancing Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with