rg/abs/2511.08916\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 164\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 20 Mar 2026 (this version, v5)]\nL9: # Title:HalluClean: A Unified Framework to Combat Hallucinations in LLMs\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Yaxin Zhao , cite7†Yu Zhang L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled HalluClean: A Unified Framework to Combat Hallucinations in LLMs, by Yaxin Zhao and 1 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Large language models (LLMs) have achieved impressive performance across a wide range of natural language processing tasks, yet they often produce hallucinated content that undermines factual reliability. To address this challenge, we introduce HalluClean, a lightweight and task-agnostic framework for detecting and correcting hallucinations in LLM-generated text.\nL17: HalluClean adopts a reasoning-enhanced paradigm, explicitly decomposing the process into planning, execution, and revision stages to identify and refine unsupported claims. It employs minimal task-routing prompts to enable zero-shot generalization across diverse domains, without relying on external knowledge sources or supervised detectors. We conduct extensive evaluations on five representative tasks-question answering, dialogue, summarization, math word problems, and contradiction detection.\nL18: Experimental results show that HalluClean significantly improves factual consistency and outperforms competitive baselines, demonstrating its potential to enhance the trustworthiness of LLM outputs in real-world applications.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2511.08916 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2511.08916v5 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.08916†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: Journal reference:  | Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence, 40(42), 36092-36100 (2026)\nL24: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | cite13†https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v40i42.40926†doi.org DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Yaxin Zhao [cite14†view email ]\nL28: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 02:50:56 UTC (1,897 KB)\nL29: cite15†[v2] Wed, 19 Nov 2025 08:22:50 UTC (1,898 KB)\nL30: cite16†[v3] Thu, 20 Nov 2025 11:42:04 UTC (1,898 KB)\nL31: cite17†[v4] Fri, 5 Dec 2025 09:31:19 UTC (1,078 KB)\nL32: [v5] Fri, 20 Mar 2026 03:02:05 UTC (1,078 KB)\nL33: \nL34: Full-text links:\nL35: ## Access Paper:\nL36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled HalluClean: A Unified Framework to Combat Hallucinations in LLMs, by Yaxin Zhao and 1 other authors\nL38: \nL39:   * cite8†View PDF L40:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L41:   * cite18†TeX Source L42: \nL43: cite19†view license L44: \nL45: ### Current browse context:\nL46: \nL47: cs.CL\nL48: \nL49: cite20†< prev |   cite21†next > L50: \nL51: cite22†new | cite23†recent | cite24†2025-11 L52: \nL53: Change to browse by:\nL54: \nL55: cite25†cs L56: ### References & Citations\nL57: \nL58:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L59:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L60:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L61: \nL62: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL63: \nL64: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL65: \nL66: [Button: ×]\nL67: \nL68: loading...\nL69: \nL70: Data provided by:\nL71: \nL72: ### Bookmark\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL75: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL76: \nL77: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL78: \nL79: Bibliographic Explorer (cite29†What is the Ex